v3.0.0 - unreleased

Main changes:
  -> Every piece of printed text can now be styled, not just the four colors of the overview.
      20 semantic tokens are exposed (heading, separator, bar-frame, number, percent, label,
      overview-label, details-language, overview-language, details-total, keyword, the four progress
      tokens, summary, success, warning, error, footer), and each takes a color plus any of bold, italic,
      underline, dim and reverse, in any order. "default" leaves the terminal's own foreground alone.
      Run "--help style" for the full list.

(Breaking!)
  -> The color palette files have a new format: every line is now a "token = value" pair, where
      "languages" holds the 1 to 5 overview colors that used to be the whole file, and any style
      token can be declared alongside it. Palettes written for older versions are detected and
      converted in place, whenever they show up, so nothing has to be done by hand.

New commands:
  -> --style <token>=<value>,... : overrides the style of one or more categories of printed text,
      for a single run. Can also be stored in a configuration file and is kept by --save.
  -> --bar-thickness slim|medium|fat|low : chooses the character the overview bar is drawn with.
      'slim' is the default and the only ASCII one, so it is the fallback for terminals that
      cannot render box drawing characters.
  -> --sort lines|files|code|size|name : chooses the order of the languages.
  -> --top <n> : shows only the first n languages, and states underneath how many were hidden.
      The total keeps counting all of them, and the overview never shows more than n either.

Change in commands:
  -> (Breaking!) The default order of the languages is now the number of lines. It used to be a
      fixed formula, 'files * 10 + bytes', in which the byte count dominated by orders of magnitude,
      so in practice the list was ordered by size while the report leads with lines. Ties are now
      broken alphabetically instead of being left to the iteration order of a hash map.
      This also changes which languages reach the overview and which fall into its 'others' entry.
      Use '--sort size' to get the old order back.
  -> --color-palette now applies the palette's overview colors and any style tokens it declares.
      --colors still overrides only the overview colors, so a palette's styles apply even when
      --colors is given.
      Styles are applied in this order: built-in defaults, then the palette, then your configuration
      file, then --style. So a palette can carry a complete look, while the styles in your own
      config survive switching between palettes.

Bugfixes:
  -> The percentage bar of the overview is now more accurate

Other:
  -> With no palette and no --style, the output is byte for byte identical to v2.0.1, escape codes
      included: the built-in defaults are exactly the appearance that used to be hardcoded.
  -> The test suite gained per-language parser fixtures that declare their own hand-verified counts,
      and a byte-exact comparison of the whole fixture corpus against a recorded result.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



v2.0.1 - 27/7/2026

  6,320 Total lines  -  4,531 Code lines

Bugfixes:
  -> Fixed the program refusing to start on a machine where it had never been executed before, but
      where the test suite had, reporting that the language dir "has been deleted". Merely reading
      the persistent paths used to create the application folder as a side effect, so the test suite
      created an empty one, which every subsequent run then mistook for a complete installation.
      The paths are now created only during the actual initialization, and an installation counts as
      complete only when the language, config and log dirs are really there.
  -> An incomplete application folder is now completed on the next execution, instead of the program
      either failing or wiping the whole folder. A missing or emptied "languages" dir is regenerated
      in place, leaving the "config" and "logs" folders untouched, and a failure in the middle of the
      initialization no longer deletes the saved configurations and logs.
  -> Fixed two tests that only passed on a machine with an already initialized application folder,
      since they wrote to the persistent config dir without creating it.

Other:
  -> The error messages about unreadable language files now suggest deleting only the "languages"
      folder, which is regenerated automatically, instead of the whole application folder.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



v2.0.0 - 26/7/2026

  6,312 Total lines  -  4,534 Code lines

Main changes:
  -> (Breaking!) The program now respects .gitignore files by default: ignored files and folders are
      skipped, with support for nested .gitignore files and negated patterns. Explicitly given
      target dirs are always traversed, even if a .gitignore would ignore them. Expect different
      (more meaningful) stats and much faster runs on directories containing build artifacts or
      dependencies. The --no-gitignore flag restores the old behavior.

Performance:
  -> Various optimizations that yielded significant performance improvements, especially when
      analyzing large directory trees. You can expect a ~2x performance increase on hot runs,
      and much more consistent run times, since the worst cases were eliminated.
      Runs on repositories are additionally much faster now, because of the .gitignore support.

Bugfixes:
  -> Fixed nondeterministic language attribution for extensions claimed by multiple language files.
      Each run could previously assign such files to a different language, changing the reported
      stats between identical runs on the same directory. Files are now always counted under the
      alphabetically first language that claims the extension.
  -> Fixed nondeterministic ordering of keyword occurrences in the results. Keywords are now
      printed in alphabetical order, stable across runs.
  -> Fixed a race condition in the parallel directory traversal and file parsing, that could slow down
      the end of a run and, in rare cases, skip files entirely.
  -> Fixed the detection of mutually exclusive --languages and --exclude-languages combinations.
      It was previously missed when the excluded list was the larger one, resulting in a misleading
      "No relevant files found" report.
  -> Fixed the rewriting of configuration and language files, which could leave stale content at the
      end of a file when the new content was shorter, corrupting it. Errors while writing files are
      also no longer silently ignored.
  -> Fixed the default configuration file never actually being loaded (the program was looking for
      a file named "default.txt.txt"), which meant that any preferences saved in it were silently
      ignored on every run.
  -> Fixed the same files being counted twice when a target path was given more than once, or when
      a target path was contained in another one, like 'mezura src, src/utils'. Such targets are
      now dropped, keeping only the topmost one.

New commands:
  -> Added the --no-gitignore flag, that disables the new default of respecting .gitignore files.
  -> Added the --colors command, that overrides the colors of the "overview" section with
      1 to 5 custom colors (hex values or standard terminal color names).
  -> Added the --color-palette command, that applies a named color palette to the "overview"
      section. 8 palettes are bundled by default (Mezura, Dracula, Nord, Gruvbox, Catppuccin,
      Sunset, Neon, Ocean), as editable files in the new "data/palettes" folder, where custom
      palettes can also be added.
  -> Added the --show-palettes command, that displays the color palettes that were found,
      each one previewed on a mock overview with its own colors.
  -> Added the --tune-palettes command, that generates an interactive HTML page with all the
      detected color palettes and opens it in the browser, where their colors can be adjusted
      with live contrast metrics and turned into ready commands.

Change in commands:
  -> The target paths (--dirs) now support glob patterns (* ? [..] {..}), which are expanded to every
      existing directory and file that they match, so 'services/*/src' is a valid target. The matches
      of a pattern are skipped if a .gitignore ignores them or if they are dotted, exactly like every
      other path that the program discovers, while the paths that are written out explicitly are
      always used.
  -> The --exclude command now supports glob patterns (* ? [..] {..}), and applies one consistent
      rule for both files and folders: a pattern without a slash matches a name at any depth, and a
      pattern with slashes matches the end of the path, anchored at path components. Previously,
      folder patterns only matched exact names or full paths, and file patterns could silently
      match mid-name path suffixes.
  -> The --threads command now accepts up to 8 producers (instead of 4) and up to 30 consumers
      (instead of 12), and the automatically chosen numbers were tuned accordingly, since
      oversubscribing the consumers hides the latency of opening files.
  -> The --changelog command now prints only the changes of the current version.
      The full version history (most recent first) is available through '--changelog full'.

Dependencies:
  -> memchr = "2.8" was introduced, for more efficient substring matching
  -> globset = "0.4.19" and ignore = "0.4.20" were introduced, for the glob patterns of the
      --exclude command and for the .gitignore support, respectively
  -> glob = "0.3.3" was introduced, for the expansion of the glob patterns of the target paths
  -> lazy_static was removed, since the standard library now covers its functionality
  -> chrono and crossbeam-deque were updated to their latest versions, and the versions of all
      the dependencies are now pinned to the ones the program is tested with

Other:
  -> 9 more language files bundled by default: Ruby, Dart, Zig, Scala, SQL, Haskell, Shell,
      Elixir and Vue.
  -> Header files are now counted under dedicated "C Header" (.h) and "C++ Header" (.hpp) languages,
      instead of being ambiguously attributed to C or C++.
  -> Project migrated to 2024 language edition and now uses some more modern features
  -> Invalid values in configuration files are no longer silently replaced:
      the program now stops and reports exactly which value, in which config file, needs fixing,
      unless a valid command line argument overrides the faulty value (a warning is shown instead).
  -> The palette tuner is also available online, without having to install anything:
      https://subamanis.github.io/mezura/palette-tuner/



